 The smiths have Hesiod. The walls have Paw-sayneeus. The folktale has two hundred versions. The elephant has real skulls, and an imaginary sailor.\n\nSo was the Cyclops real? As a one-eyed giant who eats sailors, no.\n\n\"Rude.\"\n\nBut the name is real. It was wrapped around a forge, a wall, a story, and perhaps a skull.\n\nWho can prove where the Cyclops came from?\n\n\"Nobody.\"\n\nNext time: the wind in the bag, the witch with the wand, and the other monsters of the Odyssey.\n\nOdysseus thinks the worst is behind him. He has just told a giant his name, and the giant has a father."}